Bug Title: OPENJ9_BRANCH is not as expected
Bug Description: ibmruntimes/openj9-openjdk-jdk8#36 and ibmruntimes/openj9-openjdk-jdk9#121 added OPENJ9_BRANCH, which is intended to be the branch associated with the OpenJ9 SHA being built. The branch name is resolved to `HEAD` although this is not the branch used for the build.
@JasonFengJ9

description:
In Valhalla LWVT, value class fields with the `ACC_FLATTENABLE` field modifier can be flattened.
# Instance Field Layout
The existing field layout is as follows:
++
| Doubles |
++
| Objects |
++
| Singles |
++
where `Doubles` refers to double slot or 64-bit fields, `Singles` refers to singles slot or 32-bit fields, and `Objects` refers to reference type fields, which may be 32 or 64-bit.
The new field layout includes flattened value types:
++
| Doubles |
++
| VT Doubles |
++
| Objects |
++
| VT Objects |
++
| Singles |
++
| VT Singles |
++
The existing terminology makes this somewhat confusing. `VT Doubles` refers to value type fields with the `ACC_FLATTENABLE` modifier that themselves include at least one double slot field. Similarly, `VT Objects` have no double slot fields but do have at least one reference type field, and `VT Singles` contain only single slot fields.
When a value type is flattened, it keeps only its fields. The header and lockword are removed, saving 8 bytes.
The initial implementation will choose to flatten all flattenable fields. It will also use padding liberally. Superclass backfill will not be used by value types, but can be used be regular fields (see Example2). Backfill of value types with at least one double slot field will not be used by any fields (see Example3).
The following examples use 32-bit references. The `@flattenable` annotation is temporary syntax used to denote a flattenable instance field in these examples. **It is not actual Java syntax**.
 Example 1:
class VTDouble {
 public final double theDouble;
 public final Object theObject;
 public final int theSingle;
}
class VTObject {
 public final Object theObject;
 public final int theSingle;
}
class VTSingle {
 public final int firstSingle;
 public final int secondSingle;
}
class SuperWithSingle {
 int superfield;
}
class Example1 extends SuperWithSingle {
 @flattenable VTDouble flatDouble1;
 @flattenable VTObject flatObject1;
 @flattenable VTObject flatObject2;
 @flattenable VTSingle flatSingle1;
 double double1;
 double double2;
 Object object1;
 int single1;
 int single2;
}
 ++
0 | header |
 ++
4 | lockword |
 ++
8 | Single | < int superfield
 ++
12 | Single | < int single1
 ++
16 | | < double double1
 Double 20 | |
 ++
24 | | < double double2
 Double 28 | |
 ++
32 | | < VTDouble flatDouble1
 Double 36 | |
 ++
40 | Object |
 ++
44 | Single |
 ++
48 | Object | < Object object1
 ++
52 | Object | < VTObject flatObject1
 ++
56 | Single |
 ++
60 | Object | < VTObject flatObject2
 ++
64 | Single |
 ++
68 | Single | < int single2
 ++
72 | Single | < VTSingle flatSingle1
 ++
76 | Single | ++
 Example 2:
class VTWithSingles {
 public final int a;
 public final int b;
 public final int c;
}
class SuperWithSingle {
 int superfield;
}
class Example2 extends SuperWithSingle {
 @flattenable VTWithSingles flat;
}
 ++
0 | header |
 ++
4 | lockword |
 ++
8 | Single | < int superfield
 ++
12 |///backfill///|
 ++
16 | Single | < VTWithSingles flat
 ++
20 | Single |
 ++
24 | Single |
 ++
28 |///backfill///|
 ++
In this example, it would have been possible for `flat` to use `SuperWithSingle`'s backfill.
 Example 3:
class VTWithDoubleSingle {
 public final double theDouble;
 public final int theSingle;
}
class Example3 {
 @flattenable VTWithDoubleSingle first;
 @flattenable VTWithDoubleSingle second;
 @flattenable VTWithDoubleSingle third;
 int a;
 int b;
 int c;
}
 ++
0 | header |
 ++
4 | lockword |
 ++
8 | | < VTWithDoubleSingle first
 Double 12 | |
 ++
16 | Single |
 ++
20 |////padding///|
 ++
24 | | < VTWithDoubleSingle second
 Double 28 | |
 ++
32 | Single |
 ++
36 |////padding///|
 ++
40 | | < VTWithDoubleSingle third
 Double 44 | |
 ++
48 | Single |
 ++
52 |////padding///|
 ++
56 | Single | < int a
 ++
60 | Single | < int b
 ++
64 | Single | < int c
 ++
In this example, backfill is left after the `VTWithDoubleSingle`s since the each must maintain 8-byte alignment for their double slot fields. This backfill could have been used by the single slot fields at the end.
# Computing the `totalInstanceSize`
To determine the instance size of a class with flattenable fields, we must first load the `J9Class`es of all fields marked `ACC_FLATTENABLE`. The `J9Class` gives the `totalInstanceSize` and `backfillOffset`. The `backfillOffset` is necessary to pack value types with only single slot fields.
Note that included in the `totalInstanceSize` of value class is the `totalInstanceSize` of the value class' superclass, `java.lang.Object`. This means the lockword is included in the value class' `totalInstanceSize`, and should be removed.
# Instance Description
`instanceDescription` field in `J9Class` must be updated to correctly indicate references in flattened fields.
# Updating Existing Bytecodes
Changes to must be made to `getfield` and `putfield`. When used on a flattened instance field, `getfield` should return a reference to a newly allocated copy of the field (in case the original object is destroyed before the reference to the field is lost). `putfield` should copy the fields of the source object into the destination instance field.
# Flattening Array Type Fields
