Bug Title: Increase default shared cache size for Java 8 64-bit
Bug Description: #1930 #1164 increased the default shared cache size for Java 9 and later. We should consider doing the same for Java 8. Since Java 8 may be the more often used JVM, increasing the default in Java 8 should be beneficial, especially for those enabling -Xshareclasses without understanding that typically the default size needs to be increased. We can wait a little bit and see if any problems are discovered by the change to Java 9 and later.
@hangshao0 fyi

description:
Virtual method call through reflection API may work incorrectly after base class is redefined by Java agent.
Here is the simplified description test case for the problem – we have child class (Bar) which overrides methods of its base class (Foo), then base class is redefined using Instrumentation API (Java Agent):
java
public class Foo {
 private String v;
 public void setValue(String v) { this.v = v; }
 public String getValue() { return v; }
}
public class Bar extends Foo {
 private String v;
 public void setValue(String v) { this.v = v; }
 public String getValue() { return v; }
}
...
// Redefine class Foo here (Intstrumentation.redefineClasses)
...
Foo foo = new Bar();
foo.setValue("Bar");
System.out.println("Direct getValue() = " + foo.getValue());
System.out.println("Reflection getValue() = " + Foo.class.getMethod("getValue").invoke(foo));
Expected result – same value ("Bar") is returned from both calls.
Actual result – direct call returns correct value, while call via reflection returns null.
Once Foo class is redefined (without actual changes to its bytecode), then attempt to access overridden methods of Bar class using reflection-based methods for Foo class gives wrong results – call will be dispatched to the Foo implementation instead of Bar implementation (i.e. virtual invocation is incorrectly dispatched to the super class). At the same time, direct virtual call (without using reflection) is dispatched correctly.
The problem is not reproduced under following conditions:
- If Foo.class.getMethod('getValue') call is performed before class redefinition
- If interpreter is used (-Xint)
This problem was observed on Linux x86-64 IBM Java 8u161 and is reproduced on the latest OpenJ9 jdk10 build from adoptopenjdk:
