text:
Bug Title: Reflective method invocation may work incorrectly after class redefinition 
Bug Description: Virtual method call through reflection API may work incorrectly after base class is redefined by Java agent.
Here is the simplified description test case for the problem – we have child class (Bar) which overrides methods of its base class (Foo), then base class is redefined using Instrumentation API (Java Agent):
java
public class Foo {
 private String v;
 public void setValue(String v) { this.v = v; }
 public String getValue() { return v; }
}
public class Bar extends Foo {
 private String v;
 public void setValue(String v) { this.v = v; }
 public String getValue() { return v; }
}
...
// Redefine class Foo here (Intstrumentation.redefineClasses)
...
Foo foo = new Bar();
foo.setValue("Bar");
System.out.println("Direct getValue() = " + foo.getValue());
System.out.println("Reflection getValue() = " + Foo.class.getMethod("getValue").invoke(foo));
Expected result – same value ("Bar") is returned from both calls.
Actual result – direct call returns correct value, while call via reflection returns null.
Once Foo class is redefined (without actual changes to its bytecode), then attempt to access overridden methods of Bar class using reflection-based methods for Foo class gives wrong results – call will be dispatched to the Foo implementation instead of Bar implementation (i.e. virtual invocation is incorrectly dispatched to the super class). At the same time, direct virtual call (without using reflection) is dispatched correctly.
The problem is not reproduced under following conditions:
- If Foo.class.getMethod('getValue') call is performed before class redefinition
- If interpreter is used (-Xint)
This problem was observed on Linux x86-64 IBM Java 8u161 and is reproduced on the latest OpenJ9 jdk10 build from adoptopenjdk:
openjdk version "10.0.1-adoptopenjdk" 2018-04-17
OpenJDK Runtime Environment (build 10.0.1-adoptopenjdk+0-adhoc.jenkins.openjdk) Eclipse OpenJ9 VM (build master-25d93508, JRE 10 Linux amd64-64-Bit Compressed References 20180518_54 (JIT enabled, AOT enabled)
OpenJ9 - 25d93508
OMR - 15290506
JCL - dee0290582 based on jdk-10.0.1+10)
Attached is the full test case to reproduce the problem (unzip, set path to JDK in setenv.sh, run build.sh, then run.sh): [agent-test.zip](

Bug Title: xlinux machines are out of disk space, failing PR builds
Bug Description: 1, 3, 4 failed here.
@jdekonin fyi

description:
In the case a class has more than one method matching the signature supplied to either `getDeclaredMethod` or `getMethod`, the behavior specified by the [API documentation]( is that it should return the method which has the more specific return type. Example.: for `Bar.class.getDeclaredMethod("foo")` where `Bar` is defined as :
class Bar {
 java.util.Collection foo() { return null; }
 java.util.List foo() { return null; }
}
it should return the method: ` List foo()`
The current implementation relies on `getClassDepth` to determine the most specific return type, and as `java.util.List` and `java.util.Collection` have the same depth (1) it does not make a distinction between the two methods and may return the method: `Collection foo()`, which violates the API specification..

text:
Bug Title: Class.getDeclaredMethod's behavior is not consistent with API documentation
Bug Description: In the case a class has more than one method matching the signature supplied to either `getDeclaredMethod` or `getMethod`, the behavior specified by the [API documentation]( is that it should return the method which has the more specific return type. Example.: for `Bar.class.getDeclaredMethod("foo")` where `Bar` is defined as :
class Bar {
 java.util.Collection foo() { return null; }
 java.util.List foo() { return null; }
}
it should return the method: ` List foo()`
The current implementation relies on `getClassDepth` to determine the most specific return type, and as `java.util.List` and `java.util.Collection` have the same depth (1) it does not make a distinction between the two methods and may return the method: `Collection foo()`, which violates the API specification..

