description:
Since OSX is derived from BSD Unix, it seems logical to enable `J9UNIX` on OSX.
Currently, `defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)` is used at a lot of places. There are inconsistencies where we have missed to add `defined(OSX)` at some `defined(J9UNIX)` locations. It will be cleaner to enable `J9UNIX` on OSX instead of using `defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)` everywhere.
At some places, `J9UNIX` code doesn't apply to `OSX`. With this revelation, it won't be straight forward to enable `J9UNIX` on `OSX`. Someone will have to look at all `J9UNIX` locations and evaluate if that piece of code is needed for `OSX`.
Example -`jvm.c::JVM_SetLength`
#elif defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 result = ftruncate64(fd, length);
#elif defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 result = ftruncate(fd, length);
Another case - in `jvm.c::JVM_Available`, `stat` is used on `OSX` whereas `stat64` is used on `J9UNIX`.
#if defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 struct stat64 tempStat;
#endif
#if defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 struct stat tempStat;
There may be more places where `J9UNIX` code doesn't apply to `OSX`. We need to be careful.
There is mixed opinion the possible approaches:
**Approach 1:** Some want `J9UNIX` enabled on `OSX` and special cases where `J9UNIX` doesn't apply to `OSX` handled separately.
**Approach 2:** Although there are only 34 places where `J9UNIX` is used, and fewer that don't already use `OSX` as well. It would be worth looking at the remaining places to see if `OSX` needs to be added.
Which approach should be taken?
 `J9UNIX` is used at 20 locations in OpenJ9:
1) `jvm.c::JVM_SocketShutdown` - J9UNIX and OSX execute the same code.
jint JNICALL
JVM_SocketShutdown(jint fd, jint howto)
{
 jint retVal = JNI_FALSE;
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
 retVal = shutdown(fd, howto);
#elif defined(WIN32) /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX) */
 retVal = closesocket(fd);
#else /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX) */
 assert(!"JVM_SocketShutdown() stubbed!");
#endif /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX) */
2) `j9vm_internal.h` - J9UNIX and OSX execute the same code to include header files.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <setjmp.h>
#include <sys/time.h>
#if defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
#define J9FSTAT fstat
#else /* defined(J9ZTPF) */
#define J9FSTAT fstat64
#endif /* !defined(J9ZTPF) */
#endif /* J9UNIX */
3) `jvm.c::DestroyJavaVM` - The below code is not invoked on OSX. J9UNIX and OSX should both execute the below code.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 j9vm_dllHandle = 0;
 java_dllHandle = 0;
#endif
4) `jvm.c::preloadLibraries` - J9UNIX and OSX both include definition of `preloadLibraries`. #if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX)
static BOOLEAN preloadLibraries(void)
{
 ....
}
#endif /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) */
5) `jvm.c::JNI_CreateJavaVM` - Copy `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` onto `ldLibraryPathValue`. Same code used by J9UNIX and OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
 ldLibraryPathValue = getenv(ENV_LD_LIB_PATH);
 if (NULL != ldLibraryPathValue) {
 size_t pathLength = strlen(ldLibraryPathValue) +1;
 char *envTemp = malloc(pathLength);
 if (NULL == envTemp) {
 result = JNI_ERR;
 goto exit;
 }
 strcpy(envTemp, ldLibraryPathValue);
 ldLibraryPathValue = envTemp;
 }
#endif
6) `vmargs_api.h` - On OSX, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` equivalent is `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH`. So, `ENV_LD_LIB_PATH` should be defined as follows:
#if defined(OSX)
#define ENV_LD_LIB_PATH "DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH"
#else /* defined(OSX) */
#define ENV_LD_LIB_PATH "LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
#endif /* defined(OSX) */
7) `jvm.c::JNI_CreateJavaVM` - Get `IBM_MALLOCTRACE_STR` environment variable. Same code used by J9UNIX and OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX)
 if (getenv(IBM_MALLOCTRACE_STR)) {
 ibmMallocTraceSet = TRUE;
 }
#endif
8) `jvm.c::preloadLibrary` - Derive the path to a library using the path to `libjvm.dylib` as a reference. Same code is shared between J9UNIX and OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
 buffer = jvmBufferCat(buffer, "/lib");
 buffer = jvmBufferCat(buffer, dllName);
 buffer = jvmBufferCat(buffer, J9PORT_LIBRARY_SUFFIX);
 ....
9) `jvm.c::JVM_LoadSystemLibrary` - Same code is shared between J9UNIX and OSX.
10) `jvm.c::JVM_LoadLibrary` - Code is only enabled for J9UNIX. This code should also be enabled for OSX. Code is similar to `JVM_LoadSystemLibrary`. Currently, `JVM_LoadLibrary` won't work properly on OSX.
11) `jvm.c::JVM_Available` - J9UNIX and OSX code slightly differs here.
#if defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 struct stat64 tempStat;
#endif
#if defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 struct stat tempStat;
#endif
12) `jvm.c::JVM_Available` - The below code should also be enabled on OSX. Currently, it is disabled. So, `JVM_Available` won't function properly on OSX.
#if (defined(LINUX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)) || defined(AIXPPC) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 else {
 struct pollfd pollOne;
 int ret = 0;
 pollOne.fd = descriptor;
 pollOne.events = POLLRDNORM | POLLRDBAND | POLLPRI;
 pollOne.revents = 0;
 if (-1 != poll(&pollOne, 1, 0)) {
 /* poll succeeded (-1 is failure) */
 if(0 != (pollOne.events & pollOne.revents)) {
 /* if the one descriptor we were looking at returns a modified revents
 which matches a read operation, return at least one byte readable */
 *bytes = 1;
 Trc_SC_Available_Exit(1, *bytes);
 return 1;
 } else {
 /* none of the events are ready so this is 0 bytes readable */
 *bytes = 0;
 Trc_SC_Available_Exit(1, *bytes);
 return 1;
 }
 } else {
 /* poll failed so use a poll failure trace point and return failure */
 Trc_SC_Available_poll_failed(descriptor, errno);
 *bytes = 0;
 return 0;
 }
 }
#endif
13) `jvm.c::JVM_Open` - J9UNIX and OSX code slightly differs here.
#if defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 struct stat64 tempStat;
 int doUnlink;
#endif
#if defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 struct stat tempStat;
 int doUnlink;
#endif
14) `jvm.c::JVM_Open` - J9UNIX and OSX share the same code here.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX)
#if defined(OSX)
#define EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS 0
#else
#define EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS O_LARGEFILE
#endif /* defined(OSX) */
#ifndef O_DSYNC
#define O_DSYNC O_SYNC
#endif
 doUnlink = (flags & O_TEMPORARY);
#if !defined(J9ZTPF)
 flags &= (O_CREAT | O_APPEND | O_RDONLY | O_RDWR | O_TRUNC | O_WRONLY | O_EXCL | O_NOCTTY | O_NONBLOCK | O_NDELAY | O_SYNC | O_DSYNC);
#else /* !defined(J9ZTPF) */
 flags &= (O_CREAT | O_APPEND | O_RDONLY | O_RDWR | O_TRUNC | O_WRONLY | O_EXCL | O_NOCTTY | O_NONBLOCK | O_SYNC | O_DSYNC);
#endif /* defined(J9ZTPF) */
#endif /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX) */
15) `jvm.c::JVM_Open` - Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX. Otherwise, `JVM_Open` won't work properly on OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 do {
 errorVal = 0;
#endif
#ifdef J9OS_I5
 returnVal = Xj9Open_JDK6((char *)filename, (flags | EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS), mode);
#else
 returnVal = open(filename, (flags | EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS), mode);
#endif
 if (-1 == returnVal) {
 errorVal = errno;
 }
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 /* Unix open() call does not reject directories, so extra checks required */
 if ((returnVal>=0) && (J9FSTAT(returnVal, &tempStat)1)) {
 Trc_SC_Open_fstat64(filename);
 close(returnVal);
 return -1;
 }
 if ((returnVal>=0) && S_ISDIR(tempStat.st_mode)) {
 char buf[1];
 Trc_SC_Open_isDirectory(filename);
 /* force errno to be EISDIR in case JVM_GetLastErrorString is called */
 errno = EISDIR;
 close(returnVal);
 return -1;
 }
 /* On unices, open() may return EAGAIN or EINTR, and should then be re-invoked */
 }
 while ((-1 == returnVal) && ((EAGAIN == errorVal) || (EINTR == errorVal)));
 /* Unix does not have an O_TEMPORARY flag. Unlink if Sovereign O_TEMPORARY flag passed in. */
 if ((returnVal>=0) && doUnlink)
 unlink(filename);
#endif
16) `jvm.c::JVM_SocketAvailable` - J9UNIX and OSX share the same code.
17) `jvm.c::JVM_Timeout` - J9UNIX and OSX share the same code.
18) `jvm.h` - Enable `__USE_LARGEFILE64` and add header files. Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX.
#ifdef J9UNIX
#ifndef __USE_LARGEFILE64
#define __USE_LARGEFILE64
#endif
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/param.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#endif /* J9UNIX */
19) `jvm.h` - Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX. OSX supports `sigjmp_buf`. [Reference]( `sigjmp_buf` is related to functions such as `sigsetjmp()/siglongjmp()`, which save and restore the signal mask.
#ifdef J9UNIX
typedef sigjmp_buf* pre_block_t;
#else
typedef void* pre_block_t;
#endif
20) `vmargs.c::addJavaLibraryPath`: Unused variable `envVarSize`. Remove dead code.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 IDATA envVarSize = 0;
#endif
21) `vmargs.c::addJavaLibraryPath`: Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX. `/usr/lib64` doesn't exist on OSX. The code to append `/usr/lib64` should be disabled on OSX. Only the code to append `/usr/lib` should be enabled on OSX.
#ifdef J9UNIX
#if defined(J9VM_ENV_DATA64)
 /* JAZZ103 117105: 64-bit JDKs on Linux and AIX should add /usr/lib64 to java.library.path ahead of /usr/lib. */
#define USRLIB64 ":/usr/lib64"
 substringBuffer[substringIndex] = USRLIB64;
 substringIndex += 1;
 substringLength += (sizeof(USRLIB64) - 1) ;
#undef USRLIB64
#endif /* defined(J9VM_ENV_DATA64) */
 substringBuffer[substringIndex] = ":/usr/lib";
 substringIndex += 1;
 substringLength += strlen(":/usr/lib");
#endif
 Summary
- Current locations where code is shared between J9UNIX and OSX: 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 14, 16, and 17. [10 locations]
- Locations where code differs between J9UNIX and OSX: 11 and 13. [2 locations]
- Locations where code should be shared between J9UNIX and OSX; these locations are currently disabled on OSX: 3, 10, 12, 15, 18, 19 and 21. [7 locations]
- Unrelated to J9UNIX: 6 (`LD_LIBRARY_PATH` -> `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` on OSX) and 20 (remove dead code).
In seventeen locations, J9UNIX and OSX share the same code. In two locations, J9UNIX and OSX code slightly differs. So, J9UNIX macro should be enabled on OSX. The two outliers should be separately handled with comments stating why J9UNIX and OSX code differs.

text:
Bug Title: Enable J9UNIX locations on OSX
Bug Description: Since OSX is derived from BSD Unix, it seems logical to enable `J9UNIX` on OSX.
Currently, `defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)` is used at a lot of places. There are inconsistencies where we have missed to add `defined(OSX)` at some `defined(J9UNIX)` locations. It will be cleaner to enable `J9UNIX` on OSX instead of using `defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)` everywhere.
At some places, `J9UNIX` code doesn't apply to `OSX`. With this revelation, it won't be straight forward to enable `J9UNIX` on `OSX`. Someone will have to look at all `J9UNIX` locations and evaluate if that piece of code is needed for `OSX`.
Example -`jvm.c::JVM_SetLength`
#elif defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 result = ftruncate64(fd, length);
#elif defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 result = ftruncate(fd, length);
Another case - in `jvm.c::JVM_Available`, `stat` is used on `OSX` whereas `stat64` is used on `J9UNIX`.
#if defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 struct stat64 tempStat;
#endif
#if defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 struct stat tempStat;
There may be more places where `J9UNIX` code doesn't apply to `OSX`. We need to be careful.
There is mixed opinion the possible approaches:
**Approach 1:** Some want `J9UNIX` enabled on `OSX` and special cases where `J9UNIX` doesn't apply to `OSX` handled separately.
**Approach 2:** Although there are only 34 places where `J9UNIX` is used, and fewer that don't already use `OSX` as well. It would be worth looking at the remaining places to see if `OSX` needs to be added.
Which approach should be taken?
 `J9UNIX` is used at 20 locations in OpenJ9:
1) `jvm.c::JVM_SocketShutdown` - J9UNIX and OSX execute the same code.
jint JNICALL
JVM_SocketShutdown(jint fd, jint howto)
{
 jint retVal = JNI_FALSE;
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
 retVal = shutdown(fd, howto);
#elif defined(WIN32) /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX) */
 retVal = closesocket(fd);
#else /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX) */
 assert(!"JVM_SocketShutdown() stubbed!");
#endif /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX) */
2) `j9vm_internal.h` - J9UNIX and OSX execute the same code to include header files.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
#include <sys/socket.h>
#include <dlfcn.h>
#include <sys/ioctl.h>
#include <setjmp.h>
#include <sys/time.h>
#if defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
#define J9FSTAT fstat
#else /* defined(J9ZTPF) */
#define J9FSTAT fstat64
#endif /* !defined(J9ZTPF) */
#endif /* J9UNIX */
3) `jvm.c::DestroyJavaVM` - The below code is not invoked on OSX. J9UNIX and OSX should both execute the below code.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 j9vm_dllHandle = 0;
 java_dllHandle = 0;
#endif
4) `jvm.c::preloadLibraries` - J9UNIX and OSX both include definition of `preloadLibraries`. #if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX)
static BOOLEAN preloadLibraries(void)
{
 ....
}
#endif /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) */
5) `jvm.c::JNI_CreateJavaVM` - Copy `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` onto `ldLibraryPathValue`. Same code used by J9UNIX and OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
 ldLibraryPathValue = getenv(ENV_LD_LIB_PATH);
 if (NULL != ldLibraryPathValue) {
 size_t pathLength = strlen(ldLibraryPathValue) +1;
 char *envTemp = malloc(pathLength);
 if (NULL == envTemp) {
 result = JNI_ERR;
 goto exit;
 }
 strcpy(envTemp, ldLibraryPathValue);
 ldLibraryPathValue = envTemp;
 }
#endif
6) `vmargs_api.h` - On OSX, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` equivalent is `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH`. So, `ENV_LD_LIB_PATH` should be defined as follows:
#if defined(OSX)
#define ENV_LD_LIB_PATH "DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH"
#else /* defined(OSX) */
#define ENV_LD_LIB_PATH "LD_LIBRARY_PATH"
#endif /* defined(OSX) */
7) `jvm.c::JNI_CreateJavaVM` - Get `IBM_MALLOCTRACE_STR` environment variable. Same code used by J9UNIX and OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX)
 if (getenv(IBM_MALLOCTRACE_STR)) {
 ibmMallocTraceSet = TRUE;
 }
#endif
8) `jvm.c::preloadLibrary` - Derive the path to a library using the path to `libjvm.dylib` as a reference. Same code is shared between J9UNIX and OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(OSX)
 buffer = jvmBufferCat(buffer, "/lib");
 buffer = jvmBufferCat(buffer, dllName);
 buffer = jvmBufferCat(buffer, J9PORT_LIBRARY_SUFFIX);
 ....
9) `jvm.c::JVM_LoadSystemLibrary` - Same code is shared between J9UNIX and OSX.
10) `jvm.c::JVM_LoadLibrary` - Code is only enabled for J9UNIX. This code should also be enabled for OSX. Code is similar to `JVM_LoadSystemLibrary`. Currently, `JVM_LoadLibrary` won't work properly on OSX.
11) `jvm.c::JVM_Available` - J9UNIX and OSX code slightly differs here.
#if defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 struct stat64 tempStat;
#endif
#if defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 struct stat tempStat;
#endif
12) `jvm.c::JVM_Available` - The below code should also be enabled on OSX. Currently, it is disabled. So, `JVM_Available` won't function properly on OSX.
#if (defined(LINUX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)) || defined(AIXPPC) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 else {
 struct pollfd pollOne;
 int ret = 0;
 pollOne.fd = descriptor;
 pollOne.events = POLLRDNORM | POLLRDBAND | POLLPRI;
 pollOne.revents = 0;
 if (-1 != poll(&pollOne, 1, 0)) {
 /* poll succeeded (-1 is failure) */
 if(0 != (pollOne.events & pollOne.revents)) {
 /* if the one descriptor we were looking at returns a modified revents
 which matches a read operation, return at least one byte readable */
 *bytes = 1;
 Trc_SC_Available_Exit(1, *bytes);
 return 1;
 } else {
 /* none of the events are ready so this is 0 bytes readable */
 *bytes = 0;
 Trc_SC_Available_Exit(1, *bytes);
 return 1;
 }
 } else {
 /* poll failed so use a poll failure trace point and return failure */
 Trc_SC_Available_poll_failed(descriptor, errno);
 *bytes = 0;
 return 0;
 }
 }
#endif
13) `jvm.c::JVM_Open` - J9UNIX and OSX code slightly differs here.
#if defined(J9UNIX) && !defined(J9ZTPF)
 struct stat64 tempStat;
 int doUnlink;
#endif
#if defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(J9ZTPF) || defined(OSX)
 struct stat tempStat;
 int doUnlink;
#endif
14) `jvm.c::JVM_Open` - J9UNIX and OSX share the same code here.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX)
#if defined(OSX)
#define EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS 0
#else
#define EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS O_LARGEFILE
#endif /* defined(OSX) */
#ifndef O_DSYNC
#define O_DSYNC O_SYNC
#endif
 doUnlink = (flags & O_TEMPORARY);
#if !defined(J9ZTPF)
 flags &= (O_CREAT | O_APPEND | O_RDONLY | O_RDWR | O_TRUNC | O_WRONLY | O_EXCL | O_NOCTTY | O_NONBLOCK | O_NDELAY | O_SYNC | O_DSYNC);
#else /* !defined(J9ZTPF) */
 flags &= (O_CREAT | O_APPEND | O_RDONLY | O_RDWR | O_TRUNC | O_WRONLY | O_EXCL | O_NOCTTY | O_NONBLOCK | O_SYNC | O_DSYNC);
#endif /* defined(J9ZTPF) */
#endif /* defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390) || defined(OSX) */
15) `jvm.c::JVM_Open` - Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX. Otherwise, `JVM_Open` won't work properly on OSX.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 do {
 errorVal = 0;
#endif
#ifdef J9OS_I5
 returnVal = Xj9Open_JDK6((char *)filename, (flags | EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS), mode);
#else
 returnVal = open(filename, (flags | EXTRA_OPEN_FLAGS), mode);
#endif
 if (-1 == returnVal) {
 errorVal = errno;
 }
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 /* Unix open() call does not reject directories, so extra checks required */
 if ((returnVal>=0) && (J9FSTAT(returnVal, &tempStat)1)) {
 Trc_SC_Open_fstat64(filename);
 close(returnVal);
 return -1;
 }
 if ((returnVal>=0) && S_ISDIR(tempStat.st_mode)) {
 char buf[1];
 Trc_SC_Open_isDirectory(filename);
 /* force errno to be EISDIR in case JVM_GetLastErrorString is called */
 errno = EISDIR;
 close(returnVal);
 return -1;
 }
 /* On unices, open() may return EAGAIN or EINTR, and should then be re-invoked */
 }
 while ((-1 == returnVal) && ((EAGAIN == errorVal) || (EINTR == errorVal)));
 /* Unix does not have an O_TEMPORARY flag. Unlink if Sovereign O_TEMPORARY flag passed in. */
 if ((returnVal>=0) && doUnlink)
 unlink(filename);
#endif
16) `jvm.c::JVM_SocketAvailable` - J9UNIX and OSX share the same code.
17) `jvm.c::JVM_Timeout` - J9UNIX and OSX share the same code.
18) `jvm.h` - Enable `__USE_LARGEFILE64` and add header files. Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX.
#ifdef J9UNIX
#ifndef __USE_LARGEFILE64
#define __USE_LARGEFILE64
#endif
#include <unistd.h>
#include <sys/param.h>
#include <dirent.h>
#endif /* J9UNIX */
19) `jvm.h` - Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX. OSX supports `sigjmp_buf`. [Reference]( `sigjmp_buf` is related to functions such as `sigsetjmp()/siglongjmp()`, which save and restore the signal mask.
#ifdef J9UNIX
typedef sigjmp_buf* pre_block_t;
#else
typedef void* pre_block_t;
#endif
20) `vmargs.c::addJavaLibraryPath`: Unused variable `envVarSize`. Remove dead code.
#if defined(J9UNIX) || defined(J9ZOS390)
 IDATA envVarSize = 0;
#endif
21) `vmargs.c::addJavaLibraryPath`: Currently, disabled. The below code should be enabled on OSX. `/usr/lib64` doesn't exist on OSX. The code to append `/usr/lib64` should be disabled on OSX. Only the code to append `/usr/lib` should be enabled on OSX.
#ifdef J9UNIX
#if defined(J9VM_ENV_DATA64)
 /* JAZZ103 117105: 64-bit JDKs on Linux and AIX should add /usr/lib64 to java.library.path ahead of /usr/lib. */
#define USRLIB64 ":/usr/lib64"
 substringBuffer[substringIndex] = USRLIB64;
 substringIndex += 1;
 substringLength += (sizeof(USRLIB64) - 1) ;
#undef USRLIB64
#endif /* defined(J9VM_ENV_DATA64) */
 substringBuffer[substringIndex] = ":/usr/lib";
 substringIndex += 1;
 substringLength += strlen(":/usr/lib");
#endif
 Summary
- Current locations where code is shared between J9UNIX and OSX: 1, 2, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9, 14, 16, and 17. [10 locations]
- Locations where code differs between J9UNIX and OSX: 11 and 13. [2 locations]
- Locations where code should be shared between J9UNIX and OSX; these locations are currently disabled on OSX: 3, 10, 12, 15, 18, 19 and 21. [7 locations]
- Unrelated to J9UNIX: 6 (`LD_LIBRARY_PATH` -> `DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH` on OSX) and 20 (remove dead code).
In seventeen locations, J9UNIX and OSX share the same code. In two locations, J9UNIX and OSX code slightly differs. So, J9UNIX macro should be enabled on OSX. The two outliers should be separately handled with comments stating why J9UNIX and OSX code differs.

description:
I'm not sure if this is a bug or just something not clearly specified, but it's a behaviour different from Hotspot. Here's a small example class:
import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;
import java.lang.management.OperatingSystemMXBean;
import com.sun.management.UnixOperatingSystemMXBean;
public class OsMxBean {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 OperatingSystemMXBean osMXBean = ManagementFactory.getOperatingSystemMXBean();
 if (osMXBean instanceof UnixOperatingSystemMXBean) {
 final UnixOperatingSystemMXBean unixOsMXBean = (UnixOperatingSystemMXBean) osMXBean;
 System.out.printf("ofdc : %d / Java Version %s %s / OS %s %s\n",
 unixOsMXBean.getOpenFileDescriptorCount(),
 System.getProperty("java.version"),
 System.getProperty("java.vendor"),
 System.getProperty("os.name"),
 System.getProperty("os.version"));
 }
 }
}
Here is the output from the Hotspot VM...
 $ java OsMxBean.java
 ofdc : 5 / Java Version 11 Oracle Corporation / OS Mac OS X 10.14.1
... and OpenJ9 VMs:
 $ java OsMxBean.java
 ofdc : -1 / Java Version 11.0.1 Eclipse OpenJ9 / OS Mac OS X 18.2.0
OpenJ9 returns -1 as Open File Descriptor Count, which isn't what I would expect.
Notes:
- I've opened #3969 for the slightly unexpected OS version 18.2.0
- the method `getOpenFileDescriptorCount` is used by `com.codahale.metrics.jvm.FileDescriptorRatioGauge#getRatio`. The value -1 lets fail the corresponding unit test `com.codahale.metrics.jvm.FileDescriptorRatioGaugeTest#validateFileDescriptorRatioPresenceOnNixPlatforms`

text:
Bug Title: UnixOperatingSystemMXBean#getOpenFileDescriptorCount returns -1 on macOS
Bug Description: I'm not sure if this is a bug or just something not clearly specified, but it's a behaviour different from Hotspot. Here's a small example class:
import java.lang.management.ManagementFactory;
import java.lang.management.OperatingSystemMXBean;
import com.sun.management.UnixOperatingSystemMXBean;
public class OsMxBean {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 OperatingSystemMXBean osMXBean = ManagementFactory.getOperatingSystemMXBean();
 if (osMXBean instanceof UnixOperatingSystemMXBean) {
 final UnixOperatingSystemMXBean unixOsMXBean = (UnixOperatingSystemMXBean) osMXBean;
 System.out.printf("ofdc : %d / Java Version %s %s / OS %s %s\n",
 unixOsMXBean.getOpenFileDescriptorCount(),
 System.getProperty("java.version"),
 System.getProperty("java.vendor"),
 System.getProperty("os.name"),
 System.getProperty("os.version"));
 }
 }
}
Here is the output from the Hotspot VM...
 $ java OsMxBean.java
 ofdc : 5 / Java Version 11 Oracle Corporation / OS Mac OS X 10.14.1
... and OpenJ9 VMs:
 $ java OsMxBean.java
 ofdc : -1 / Java Version 11.0.1 Eclipse OpenJ9 / OS Mac OS X 18.2.0
OpenJ9 returns -1 as Open File Descriptor Count, which isn't what I would expect.
Notes:
- I've opened #3969 for the slightly unexpected OS version 18.2.0
- the method `getOpenFileDescriptorCount` is used by `com.codahale.metrics.jvm.FileDescriptorRatioGauge#getRatio`. The value -1 lets fail the corresponding unit test `com.codahale.metrics.jvm.FileDescriptorRatioGaugeTest#validateFileDescriptorRatioPresenceOnNixPlatforms`

description:
Not sure if this is the correct project to report this problem.
I am testing OpenJ9 (downloaded from AdoptOpenJDK) on an application that we usually run with OpenJDK.
By now the only problem we have found is related to an weird usage of Unsafe.
J9 crashes immediatelly with a `Unhandled exception Type=Segmentation error vmState=<hex>`.
The following program reproduces the problem:
java
import java.lang.reflect.Field;
import sun.misc.Unsafe;
import java.util.Random;
public class OddObject {
 public static void main(final String[] args)
 throws Exception {
 final int writeOffset = Integer.parseInt(args[0]);
 final int readOffset = Integer.parseInt(args[1]);
 final Random rnd = new Random();
 final long init = offset(NodeLong.class, "x");
 System.out.println("Init " + init);
 for (long i = 0; ; i++) {
 final NodeLong node = new NodeLong();
 theUnsafe.putLong(node, init+writeOffset, rnd.nextLong());
 System.out.println(theUnsafe.getLong(node, init+readOffset));
 }
 }
 public static final sun.misc.Unsafe theUnsafe;
 static {
 try {
 theUnsafe = (Unsafe) staticObject(
 sun.misc.Unsafe.class, "theUnsafe");
 } catch (Exception e) {
 throw new RuntimeException(e);
 }
 }
 protected static Object staticObject(final Class klass, final String field)
 throws IllegalAccessException, NoSuchFieldException {
 final Field f = klass.getDeclaredField(field);
 f.setAccessible(true);
 return f.get(null);
 }
 protected static long offset(final Class klass, final String field)
 throws Exception {
 return theUnsafe.objectFieldOffset(klass.getDeclaredField(field));
 }
 public static class NodeLong {
 public long x;
 public long y;
 }
}
When called with *even* arguments, it runs without errors.
But if any argument is *odd*, it fails with the following error:
$ java OddObject 1 0
Offset 8
Unhandled exception
