description:
The core test method looks like this:
 @Test
 public void testInnerPreexistence() {
 timer.schedule(new NotifyTask(TASK_LOAD_C, this), 8000);
 while (keepOnGoing)
 foo();
 AssertJUnit.assertTrue(foo() == 'c');
 }
So a NotifyTask is being scheduled with a 8 seconds delay and a loop is entered based on `keepOnGoing`. Looking at wakeUp we see:
 //Should not be run as a test, therefore it is disabled
 @Test(enabled = false)
 public void wakeUp(int eventId) {
 someB = new C();
 dummySync(); // to ensure correct ordering on weak memory systems
 keepOnGoing = false;
 }
And dymmySync() is as follows:
 private synchronized void dummySync() {}
There seems to be an incorrect assumption that a synchronized method has some effect visibility of `keepOnGoing`. This test might spin forever because the change to `keepOnGoing` will never be visible:
 while (keepOnGoing)
 foo();
In fact, this can be reproduced on a hotspot JVM as follows:
 $ git clone $ cd inner-preexisting-reproducer
 $ javac InnerPreexistence.java
 $ java -showversion InnerPreexistence
 openjdk version "1.8.0_191"
 OpenJDK Runtime Environment (build 1.8.0_191-b12)
 OpenJDK 64-Bit Server VM (build 25.191-b12, mixed mode)
 DEBUG: Waking up: 1
 <hangs forever>

