description:
Background
Currently, DDR only knows about two kinds of instance fields, primitives (Z, B, S, I, J, C, F, D) and L-types (LFoo;). With the introduction of valueTypes there is an additional kind of instance field called a Q-type (QFooVal;). Q-types are unique in that they may be flattened in place inside of the container. This means that unlike L-types they are not always guaranteed to be reference sized (32bit/64bit) they they can be any size. The common way to look at objects in DDR is with the `!j9object [objAddress]` command. Given a the types:
value Point2D {
 int x;
 int y;
}
value Line2D {
 Point2D st;
 Point2D en;
}
value FlattenedLine2D {
 flattened Point2D st;
 flattened Point2D en;
}
Inspecting a `Line2D` instance in today's DDR would look something like:
> !j9object <hex>
!J9Object <hex> {
 struct J9Class* clazz = !j9class <hex> // Line2D
 Object flags = <hex>;
 LPoint2D; st = !fj9object <hex> (offset=0) (Line2D)
 LPoint2D; en = !fj9object <hex> (offset=4) (Line2D)
}
The `!fj9object` denotes that the field is stored as a compressed reference. In the example above `st` and `en` contain the same value. Running `!fj9object <hex>` would output:
> !fj9object <hex>
!J9Object <hex> {
 struct J9Class* clazz = !j9class <hex> // Point2D
 Object flags = <hex>;
 I x = <hex> (offset=0) (Point2D)
 I y = <hex> (offset=4) (Point2D)
}
With Q-types the JVM is allowed to flatten the value in place, similar to a nested C struct. In DDR flattened q-types currently look like this:
> !j9object <hex>
!J9Object <hex> {
 struct J9Class* clazz = !j9class <hex> // FlattenedLine2D
 Object flags = <hex>;
 QPoint2D; st = !fj9object <hex> (offset=0) (FlattenedLine2D)
 QPoint2D; en = !fj9object <hex> (offset=4) (FlattenedLine2D)
}
Instead of showing the address of `st` and `en` it shows the contents of `st`. The examples above show what DDR currently outputs. The goal of this Issue is to add support for flattened objects.
1) Add DDR support of flattened value types
The first task is to create a command that can print q-types. Q-types may or may not be flattened. And whether a q-type is flattened or not will vary from type to type. In order to determine if a field is flattened, one has to check the `J9Class->classFlags` of that field for the `J9ClassIsFlattened` bit.
The following shows the memory layout of `FlattenedLine2D`:
<hex>: 009f1d00 // J9Class/flags
<hex>: ffeeffee // st.x
<hex>: aabbaabb // st.y
<hex>: ccddccdd // en.x
<hex>: aaffaaff //en.y
One will notice that the flattened types do not have headers. This means that the command used to print the flattened type must have a reference to the container as well as a value to indicate which field is to be printed.
New command: `flattenedj9object`
Usage: !flattenedj9object [addressOfContainer],[[fieldNum] ... ]
addressOfContainer - Address of the class/value that contains the flattened valueType field. In the case of multi-level field flattening, `addressOfContainer` is the address of the top most container. In other words, `addressOfContainer` must always point at something with a header.
fieldNum - The index of the field that is to be printed. In the case of multi-level flattening, mutliple field numbers may have to be specified.
i.e
> !j9object <hex>
!J9Object <hex> {
 struct J9Class* clazz = !j9class <hex> // FlattenedLine2D
 Object flags = <hex>;
 QPoint2D; st = !flattenedj9object <hex>,0 (offset=0) (Point2D)
 QPoint2D; en = !flattenedj9object <hex>,1 (offset=8) (Point2D)
}
If `FlattenedLine2D` where nested in another value type, then the command to print the `st` field would be ` !flattenedj9object <hex>ONTAINERADDRESS,0,0`. Where `<hex>ONTAINERADDRESS` is the address of the object that contains the `FlattenedLine2D` instance, the first `0` is the field index for the `FlattenedLine2D` instance and the last `0` is the index for the `st` field. 2) Update !j9classshape command
The class shape command prints out the names and offsets of each field. This will have to be updated to work for flattened value types.

