array class java.lang.Object[][] (no class loader)
(There are more - ~100 array classes in total).
How come OpenJ9 doesn't include array classes among visible classes?

description:
There is an issue with `recreateRAMClasses` in hshelp. When we are doing redefinition we call `determineClassesToRecreate` to scan the list of class pairs, then we add the class pairs to a hashtable. Each entry in the hashtable consists of the original ram class, and the replacement class (union: can be either ram or rom class), the replacement class is initially the new romClass. In `recreateRAMClasses` we create a copy of the classpairs in an array from the classHashtable, we sort that array and create ramclasses for each of the replacement romClasses. Once the ramClass is created we overwrite the replacement.romClass with the new ramclass.
The problem is, if we fail to create the ramClass (ie. OOM) we simply free the array (copy of hashtable) and return. The entries still exist in the original classhashtable.
 if (NULL == replacementRAMClass) {
outOfMemory:
 if (!fastHCR) {
 vmFuncs->hashClassTableAtPut(currentThread, classLoader, J9UTF8_DATA(className), J9UTF8_LENGTH(className), originalRAMClass);
 while (i != 0) {
 replaceInAllClassLoaders(currentThread, classPairs[i]->replacementClass.ramClass, classPairs[i]->originalRAMClass);
 --i;
 }
 }
 j9mem_free_memory(classPairs);
 return JVMTI_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
 }
In `redefineClassesCommon` (jvmticlass.c) if `recreateRAMClasses` fails we still notify GC of class replacement. This is a problem because we end up sending the GC the old ramClass and the new romclass.

text:
Bug Title: Redefining classes incorrectly handles failure to recreate new ramClasses
Bug Description: There is an issue with `recreateRAMClasses` in hshelp. When we are doing redefinition we call `determineClassesToRecreate` to scan the list of class pairs, then we add the class pairs to a hashtable. Each entry in the hashtable consists of the original ram class, and the replacement class (union: can be either ram or rom class), the replacement class is initially the new romClass. In `recreateRAMClasses` we create a copy of the classpairs in an array from the classHashtable, we sort that array and create ramclasses for each of the replacement romClasses. Once the ramClass is created we overwrite the replacement.romClass with the new ramclass.
The problem is, if we fail to create the ramClass (ie. OOM) we simply free the array (copy of hashtable) and return. The entries still exist in the original classhashtable.
 if (NULL == replacementRAMClass) {
outOfMemory:
 if (!fastHCR) {
 vmFuncs->hashClassTableAtPut(currentThread, classLoader, J9UTF8_DATA(className), J9UTF8_LENGTH(className), originalRAMClass);
 while (i != 0) {
 replaceInAllClassLoaders(currentThread, classPairs[i]->replacementClass.ramClass, classPairs[i]->originalRAMClass);
 --i;
 }
 }
 j9mem_free_memory(classPairs);
 return JVMTI_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY;
 }
In `redefineClassesCommon` (jvmticlass.c) if `recreateRAMClasses` fails we still notify GC of class replacement. This is a problem because we end up sending the GC the old ramClass and the new romclass.

description:
Currently, we tend to call `internalFindClassString` after calling `verifyQualifiedName`, both of which call `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc`. Normally, `internalFindClassString` has a fast path which checks the classHashTable to see if the class has already been loaded via `hashClassTableAtString`. So we propose to move the `verifyQualifiedName` call into `internalFindClassString` and do the check after the `hashClassTableAtString` so as to avoid calling `verifyQualifiedName` when duplicated classes being checked.
e.g.
internalFindClassString(J9VMThread* currentThread, j9object_t moduleName, j9object_t className, J9ClassLoader* classLoader, UDATA options)
{
 J9CfrConstantPoolInfo classNameInfo = {0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, 0};
 ...
 result = hashClassTableAtString(classLoader, (j9object_t) className); <
 if (!fastMode) {
 omrthread_monitor_exit(vm->classTableMutex);
 }
 if (NULL == result) {
 ...
 classNameInfo.bytes= copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc(currentThread, className,...., &utf8Length)
 classNameInfo.slot1 = (U_32)utf8Length;
 if (CLASSNAME_INVALID == verifyQualifiedName(_currentThread, classNameInfo)) { <
 ...
To avoid calling `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc` twice, we can replace the parameters of `verifyQualifiedName` called ` j9object_t className` with `J9CfrConstantPoolInfo classNameInfo` so as to pass it over to `verifyQualifiedName` after `classNameInfo` is set up with `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc`, in which case the results from `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc` can be shared by both `verifyQualifiedName` and the remaining code in `internalFindClassString`.
Now the question is whether there is any potential issue or technical obstacle we are not aware of.
The proposal includes the following PRs:
(1) step `[1]`: the changes of `moving verifyQualifiedName into internalFindClassString` at (2) step `[2]` & `[3]`: the changes of `Move verifyQualifiedName to jvmDefineClassHelper & defineClassCommon` at (3) step `[4]`: the changes of `share the memory allocated by
 copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc between verifyQualifiedName and its callers` at FYI: @DanHeidinga , @gacholio, @pshipton

