Bug Title: Calling hashClassTableAtString first to avoid verifying duplicate class names by verifyQualifiedName
Bug Description: Currently, we tend to call `internalFindClassString` after calling `verifyQualifiedName`, both of which call `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc`. Normally, `internalFindClassString` has a fast path which checks the classHashTable to see if the class has already been loaded via `hashClassTableAtString`. So we propose to move the `verifyQualifiedName` call into `internalFindClassString` and do the check after the `hashClassTableAtString` so as to avoid calling `verifyQualifiedName` when duplicated classes being checked.
e.g.
internalFindClassString(J9VMThread* currentThread, j9object_t moduleName, j9object_t className, J9ClassLoader* classLoader, UDATA options)
{
 J9CfrConstantPoolInfo classNameInfo = {0, 0, 0, 0, 0, NULL, 0};
 ...
 result = hashClassTableAtString(classLoader, (j9object_t) className); <
 if (!fastMode) {
 omrthread_monitor_exit(vm->classTableMutex);
 }
 if (NULL == result) {
 ...
 classNameInfo.bytes= copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc(currentThread, className,...., &utf8Length)
 classNameInfo.slot1 = (U_32)utf8Length;
 if (CLASSNAME_INVALID == verifyQualifiedName(_currentThread, classNameInfo)) { <
 ...
To avoid calling `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc` twice, we can replace the parameters of `verifyQualifiedName` called ` j9object_t className` with `J9CfrConstantPoolInfo classNameInfo` so as to pass it over to `verifyQualifiedName` after `classNameInfo` is set up with `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc`, in which case the results from `copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc` can be shared by both `verifyQualifiedName` and the remaining code in `internalFindClassString`.
Now the question is whether there is any potential issue or technical obstacle we are not aware of.
The proposal includes the following PRs:
(1) step `[1]`: the changes of `moving verifyQualifiedName into internalFindClassString` at (2) step `[2]` & `[3]`: the changes of `Move verifyQualifiedName to jvmDefineClassHelper & defineClassCommon` at (3) step `[4]`: the changes of `share the memory allocated by
 copyStringToUTF8WithMemAlloc between verifyQualifiedName and its callers` at FYI: @DanHeidinga , @gacholio, @pshipton

description:
There seems to be a regression introduced with 0.21 that results in process not terminating after user code calls System.exit in a daemon thread.
Probably related to:
I can reproduce this with the following code:
 public class Launchee {
 public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
 final CountDownLatch latch = new CountDownLatch(1);
 Thread t = new Thread() {
 @Override
 public void run() {
 latch.countDown();
 System.exit(1);
 }
 };
 t.setDaemon(true);
 t.start();
 latch.await();
 }
 }
 This sometimes hangs when executed with AdoptOpenJDK OpenJ9 8.0.262.10. Similar problem was observed with 11.0.8.10.0 and 14.0.2.12.0.
I simply launched this in a loop:
 public class Launcher {
 public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
 int i = 0;
 while (true) {
 System.out.println(i++);
 List<String> commandLine = new ArrayList<>();
 commandLine.add("adoptopenjdk-openj9_64-8.0.262.10.0\\bin\\java.exe");
 commandLine.add("-cp");
 commandLine.add("bin");
 commandLine.add("Launchee");
 ProcessBuilder pb = new ProcessBuilder(commandLine);
 pb.inheritIO();
 Process process = pb.start();
 process.waitFor();
 }
 } }
With Hotspot and previous JVMs I get
 0
 1
 2
 ...
With OpenJ9 0.21 I sometimes just get:
 0
and then it hangs forever.

