description:
Summary
We found that the order of super interface initialization in J9 is strange, which is inconsistent with other JVMs. The simplified test case can be found below:
public class Test {
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 C c = new C();
 }
 static boolean out(Class c) {
 System.out.println("initializing " + c.getName());
 return true;
 }
}
interface A {
 boolean v = Test.out(A.class);
 default void default_method(){}
}
interface B extends A {
 boolean v = Test.out(B.class);
 @Override
 default void default_method(){}
}
class C implements B {
 static boolean v = Test.out(C.class);
}
In this test case, class C implements interface B, and interface B extends interface A and overrides its default_method. The initialization order of J9 in this example is B, A, C, and other JVMs such as hotspot, the initialization order is A, B, C. How to reproduce?
Run the test case above.
