Exception in thread "main" java.lang.UnsatisfiedLinkError: fontmanager (rtld: 0712-001 Symbol _ZN2hb8vtable_tI8hb_set_tXadL_Z16hb_set_get_emptyEEXadL_Z16hb_set_referenceEEXadL_Z14hb_set_destroyEEXadL_Z20hb_set_set_user_dataEEXadL_Z20hb_set_get_user_dataEEE7destroyE was referenced
 from module /home/jenkins/peter/jdk/lib/libfontmanager.so(), but a runtime definition
 of the symbol was not found.)
Even on the same machine where the JVM was compiled it doesn't work, with the same error.
Seems like a problem with xlc 16.1.0. I think what's happening is that harfbuzz was updated from 2.8 to 4.4.1 and it no longer works with xlc 16.1.0.

description:
This issue was found with IBM Java, in a scenario where a customer sets a system wide default `-Dfile.encoding` option in the IBM_JAVA_OPTIONS environment variable, and applications can override the default by specifying their own `-Dfile.encoding` option on their command line. This works because the second `-Dfile.encoding` option on the command line supercedes the first.
Simple testcase:
public class SysPropTest {
 public static void main (String[] args) {
 if (args.length != 1) {
 System.err.println("Usage: java SysPropTest <system property name>");
 System.exit(1);
 }
 System.out.println(args[0] + " = " + System.getProperty(args[0]));
 }
}
This works as expected up until 8.0.7.5:
> java -Dfile.encoding="UTF-8" -Dfile.encoding="CP1141" SysPropTest file.encoding
file.encoding = CP1141
But with 8.0.7.6 and later, the _first_ `-Dfile.encoding` property is picked up instead, and the second one is ignored.
> java -Dfile.encoding="UTF-8" -Dfile.encoding="CP1141" SysPropTest file.encoding
file.encoding = UTF-8
I believe the patch for #14285 is responsible: The system properties are held in a Properties instance in the java.lang.System class, and prior to the patch above, the `-Dfile.encoding` options (along with any other `-D` options on the command line) were added to the Properties instance in order, from left to right, so that if two options specifying the same property key were on the command line, the second one would replace the first.
However, for reasons that aren't completely clear to me, the patch changes the code so that it gets the value of the `-Dfile.encoding` option before the System class's Properties instance is populated, and then adds that cached value to the Properties instance after it has been populated, thus replacing the value for the `file.encoding` property.
This is a problem because the cached value is obtained using this function:
Which goes through the system property options on the command line from left to right and returns the **first** one it finds, not the last one. As a result, after the patch for #14285 is applied, the first `-Dfile.encoding` option is picked up instead of the second one.

text:
Bug Title: Wrong -Dfile.encoding option picked up after #14285
Bug Description: This issue was found with IBM Java, in a scenario where a customer sets a system wide default `-Dfile.encoding` option in the IBM_JAVA_OPTIONS environment variable, and applications can override the default by specifying their own `-Dfile.encoding` option on their command line. This works because the second `-Dfile.encoding` option on the command line supercedes the first.
Simple testcase:
public class SysPropTest {
 public static void main (String[] args) {
 if (args.length != 1) {
 System.err.println("Usage: java SysPropTest <system property name>");
 System.exit(1);
 }
 System.out.println(args[0] + " = " + System.getProperty(args[0]));
 }
}
This works as expected up until 8.0.7.5:
> java -Dfile.encoding="UTF-8" -Dfile.encoding="CP1141" SysPropTest file.encoding
file.encoding = CP1141
But with 8.0.7.6 and later, the _first_ `-Dfile.encoding` property is picked up instead, and the second one is ignored.
> java -Dfile.encoding="UTF-8" -Dfile.encoding="CP1141" SysPropTest file.encoding
file.encoding = UTF-8
I believe the patch for #14285 is responsible: The system properties are held in a Properties instance in the java.lang.System class, and prior to the patch above, the `-Dfile.encoding` options (along with any other `-D` options on the command line) were added to the Properties instance in order, from left to right, so that if two options specifying the same property key were on the command line, the second one would replace the first.
However, for reasons that aren't completely clear to me, the patch changes the code so that it gets the value of the `-Dfile.encoding` option before the System class's Properties instance is populated, and then adds that cached value to the Properties instance after it has been populated, thus replacing the value for the `file.encoding` property.
This is a problem because the cached value is obtained using this function:
Which goes through the system property options on the command line from left to right and returns the **first** one it finds, not the last one. As a result, after the patch for #14285 is applied, the first `-Dfile.encoding` option is picked up instead of the second one.

description:
In JVMTI (and few instances outside JVMTI), there are occurrences of incomplete null checks for JNI-refs. Example:
jobject ref = ...;
...
if (NULL == ref) { /* Incomplete */
 ...
It's legal to have a JNI ref to NULL (think weak global ref).
A complete null check will be as follows:
jobject ref = ...;
...
j9object_t obj = (NULL == ref) ? NULL : J9_JNI_UNWRAP_REFERENCE(ref);
if (NULL == obj) {
 ...

