description:
I am seeing a strange behavior of stack walker with Java19: While running AcmeAir with `-Xjit:verbose{sampling}` the JIT sampler reports many ticks in interpreted bodies, even though those corresponding methods have been compiled. E.g.
#SAMPLE: (174205) Interpreted com/ibm/ws/webcontainer/srt/SRTServletRequest.isAsyncStarted()Z counter = XX (long running?)
Typically this is a sign of being stuck in a long running loop of a method and DLT (dynamic loop transfer) should help in this situation. However, in this case, the methods that are reported as interpreted do not have any loops. Moreover, I tried to compile from the very first invocation with `-Xjit:{com/ibm/ws/webcontainer/srt/SRTServletRequest.isAsyncStarted()Z}(count=0)` (a known hack for methods that suffer from long invocations), but the sampler still reports ticks in interpreted bodies. I have also disabled OSR and pre-existence, the two mechanisms that can move a method from jitted back to interpreted, but the strange behavior is still present.
The JIT sampler uses the stack walker to find the method at the top of the stack; this makes me think that stack walker may be incorrectly reporting some methods as interpreted when in fact they are jitted. Stack walking for sampling is performed with the following parameteres:
 walkState.flags = J9_STACKWALK_SKIP_INLINES | J9_STACKWALK_VISIBLE_ONLY | J9_STACKWALK_INCLUDE_NATIVES | J9_STACKWALK_COUNT_SPECIFIED;
 walkState.skipCount = 0;
 walkState.maxFrames = 1;
 walkState.walkThread = vmThread;
 vmThread->javaVM->walkStackFrames(vmThread, &walkState);
I should also add that I am only seeing this behavior with Java19, but not with Java17 or 8.

text:
